Understanding the Vulnerability
Brute-force attacks rely on submitting many authentication attempts until the correct password is found.
To defend against this, many applications implement rate limiting or temporarily block requests after several failed login attempts.
In this lab, the application attempted to identify the source of repeated requests and enforce an IP-based block.
The important lesson is that **security controls are only as strong as the assumptions they make**.

If an application incorrectly trusts information used to identify a client, the protection itself can become ineffective.
The vulnerability was therefore not in password authentication.
It was in the application's defensive logic.

My Biggest Challenge

Surprisingly, the vulnerability itself was not the hardest part of this lab.

The biggest challenge was understanding the automation behind the attack.





Unlike previous labs where most of the work happened inside Burp Suite, this exercise required interacting with a Python script.
As someone who has only recently started learning programming, this pushed me outside my comfort zone.

I wasn't trying to become a Python developer overnight.

Instead, I focused on understanding what the script was doing.

Questions I kept asking myself included:

* What is each variable storing?
* How does the loop move through passwords?
